\name{free_polygon}
\alias{free_polygon}
\title{
Draw a free polygon
}
\description{
Draw a closed polygon through canvas-coordinate vertices.
}
\usage{
free_polygon(points, mode = "relative", fill_color = NA, line_color = "black",
    line_thickness = 1, line_type = "solid")
}
\arguments{
\item{points}{Required finite numeric matrix with two columns (x, y) and at least three rows. Rows are successive vertices, in the unit selected by mode.}
\item{mode}{One string: \code{"relative"} (default; canvas width and height are 1), \code{"percent"} (width and height are 100), or \code{"pixel"} (logical pixels). Only positions and geometric widths/heights use this unit; font sizes, gaps and stroke widths remain logical pixels.}
\item{fill_color}{Fill color. Default \code{NA} leaves the interior unfilled. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
\item{line_color}{Stroke color. Default \code{"black"}; \code{NA} disables the stroke. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
\item{line_thickness}{One finite nonnegative stroke width in logical pixels. Default 1; 0 hides the stroke.}
\item{line_type}{One string: \code{"solid"} (default), \code{"dashed"}, or \code{"dotted"}.}
}
\details{
The last vertex is automatically connected to the first; repeating the first row is unnecessary. Fill and outline can be disabled independently. Coordinates refer to the entire logical canvas, including margins, not to tree or branch coordinates. The origin is the upper-left corner; x increases rightwards and y downwards. Values outside the canvas are not clamped.
}
\value{
A \code{yytree_layer} value; add it to a plot with \code{+}.
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{free_rectangle}}, \code{\link{free_bezier_ring}}, \code{\link{yytree}}
}
\examples{
vertices <- rbind(c(.7, .8), c(.8, .9), c(.6, .9))
p <- yytree("(A:1,B:2);") + free_polygon(vertices, fill_color = "gold")

}
